**Design.** Entities: `enum PieceType {X, O}`, `Player(name, piece)`, `Board(n)`, `Game` with `playGame()`. Keep rendering and input out of `Board` so it can be tested.

**Board logic.** Store a grid of `PieceType` (or null). `place(row, col, piece)` rejects out-of-range or occupied cells. To detect a win in O(1) per move instead of scanning the grid, keep counters: `rows[n]`, `cols[n]`, `diag`, `antiDiag`, adding +1 for one player and -1 for the other. After a move by a player the game is won if any affected counter has absolute value `n`. A draw is when `moves == n * n` without a winner.

```java
boolean move(int r, int c, int player) { // player is +1 or -1
    rows[r] += player; cols[c] += player;
    if (r == c) diag += player;
    if (r + c == n - 1) anti += player;
    return Math.abs(rows[r]) == n || Math.abs(cols[c]) == n
        || Math.abs(diag) == n || Math.abs(anti) == n;
}
```
**Game loop.** `playGame()`: alternate players from a queue (poll, play, offer back), read a move, validate it, apply it, check win or draw, and print the board. Handle invalid input by asking again, not by skipping the turn.

**Extensions.** N by N boards, more than 2 players (use a distinct id per player, and sum counters per player in maps), undo via a move stack, an AI opponent through a `MoveStrategy` interface (random, then minimax), and different win lengths.

